 "notes": "Libretto by Lorenzo Da Ponte (1790). Words follow the Peters engraving's spelling and punctuation (fù, sù, fè, pocchi, Per Bacco as two words, s'e diportata without the accent; all' amor, ell' è, poch' ore and quest' è as two words each; Ferrando's Ah non ci ho neppur dubbio, where the libretto has Oh; the quotation marks round Pura come colomba … si serba; Guglielmo's asides in brackets). Ferrando's pocchi (bar 81) is a print slip for pochi: the word keeps the print's letters, and its IPA and meaning are those of pochi. These words are sung in more syllables than the draft's IPA shows, and their IPA follows the score: Ferrando's Dei and vuoi (bars 54 and 57), Guglielmo's vai (bar 67) and saprei (bar 91). IPA, word meanings and translations come from the app's reviewed Così libretto drafts (L53, IPA checked against Castel). IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, elisions (two words on one note in the score) joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ. Translation literal, for meaning.",
